{"question":"What does wajadaka mean and how does it work here?","words":["وَوَجَدَكَ"],"type":"meaning","positions":[{"position":"God encountered or perceived the Prophet in a given condition.","reasons":"Ibn al-Jawzī gives the root as encountering or sensing something previously unperceived, and notes usage for lost property, grief, anger, and financial means; Ibn Fāris cites poetry for anger. Ibn ʿĀshūr says the verb takes one object with ḍāllan as a circumstantial state; another lexical face is seeing.","rows":["IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p615/r1","IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p615/r2","IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p615/r3","IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p615/r4","IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p615/r9","IBNASHUR-FULL:v30p398/r5"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The Prophet was the beloved sought by God in pre-eternity.","reasons":"Ibn ʿAṭāʾ says finding follows seeking and makes the Prophet the sought beloved, an envoy through whom orphans, the lost, and the needy benefit.","rows":["SULAMI:v2p400#5/r1","SULAMI:v2p400#5/r3"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Finding represents God's selection of a rare, valuable person.","reasons":"Elmalılı prefers istifā, divine selection, as the rhetorical force repeated over the three verses.","rows":["ELMALILI:93:6-8/r6"],"prefer":["ELMALILI:93:6-8/r6"],"against":[]},{"position":"Finding emphasizes the Prophet's inward state and reassurance.","reasons":"Bint al-Shāṭiʾ calls wajada a heart verb that foregrounds emotional reassurance.","rows":["BINTSHATI:v1p51/r3"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Whether finding is encounter, perception, inward recognition, or divine selection."}
{"question":"What does ḍāllan mean in Arabic usage?","words":["ضَآلًّۭا"],"type":"meaning","positions":[{"position":"A lone desert tree can be called ḍālla because travelers find their way by it.","reasons":"Al-Thaʿlabī, al-Jishumī, al-Rāzī, and al-Qurṭubī use this Arab idiom in their interpretation; al-Rāzī parallels the saying that wisdom is a believer's lost property.","rows":["THALABI:v10p228/r2","JISHUMI:v10p7457/r5","RAZI:93:7/r17","RAZI:93:7/r18","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r5","QURTUBI:93:7/r13","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7#2/r8"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"It can mean intense love or attachment.","reasons":"Al-Sulamī cites 12:95; al-Thaʿlabī cites 12:8 and 12:95; al-Māwardī adds two poetry lines. Al-Qurṭubī and Bint al-Shāṭiʾ add Qurʾānic and poetic usage, including 12:30.","rows":["SULAMI:v2p400#5/r8","THALABI:v10p228/r6","MAWARDI:93:7/r9","QURTUBI:93:7/r20","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7/r12","BINTSHATI:v1p45/r4","BINTSHATI:v1p46/r1"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"It can mean forgetting or being unaware.","reasons":"Thaʿlab cites the debt verse about one witness reminding another; al-Māwardī, al-Qurṭubī, al-Ṭabāṭabāʾī and Bint al-Shāṭiʾ also invoke 2:282. Al-Qurṭubī compares 20:52 and 12:3.","rows":["IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p408/r9","MAWARDI:93:7/r5","TABATABAI:93:8-11/r2","TABATABAI:93:8-11/r3","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7/r1","QURTUBI:93:7/r1","BINTSHATI:v1p46/r1"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"It can denote something hidden, submerged, or indistinguishable.","reasons":"Al-Rāzī cites an idiom of submerged water; al-Qurṭubī uses 32:10 for being lost in earth at burial. Bursawī also records the sense absent.","rows":["RAZI:93:7/r16","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r4","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7#3/r1","BURSEVI:v10p352#3/r3"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"It means losing a path or being uncertain how to reach a desired destination.","reasons":"Ibn Qutayba includes confusion, departure from truth, or leaving a road. Ibn ʿĀshūr distinguishes a wrong route from uncertainty and intends the latter. Bint al-Shāṭiʾ traces spatial usage to metaphorical and religious usage; Qurʾānic association with paths and 32:10 preserve the physical sense.","rows":["IBNQUTAYBA-MUSHKIL:v1p254/r1","IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p408/r6","IBNASHUR:93:7/r1","BINTSHATI:v1p44/r1","BINTSHATI:v1p44/r2"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"It can denote any departure from a straight way, slight or grave, intentional or accidental.","reasons":"Al-Rāghib's definition, cited by Bursawī and Bint al-Shāṭiʾ, permits different senses for prophets and unbelievers.","rows":["BURSEVI:v10p352#3/r4","BINTSHATI:v1p46/r4"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"The word's range includes losing a path, uncertainty, forgetfulness, love, obscurity, and religious deviation."}
{"question":"What state does 'He found you ḍāllan' describe?","words":["وَوَجَدَكَ","ضَآلًّۭا"],"type":"interpretation","positions":[{"position":"He did not yet know revelation, prophetic signs, or religious law, and God taught him.","reasons":"Al-Zajjāj and many later authors cite 42:52; others cite 12:3, 2:282, and 26:20. Al-Bayḍāwī and al-Biqāʿī add inspiration and sound reasoning; Bursawī says reason alone cannot discover these rulings. Al-Biqāʿī calls lack of guidance the effect of lacking a teacher. Al-Ṭabrisī links teaching seekers in 93:10 to this favor.","rows":["ZAJJAJ:v5p339#2/r1","THALABI:v10p225/r7","MAWARDI:93:7/r1","WAHIDI-BASIT:v24p109#2/r1","WAHIDI-BASIT:v24p109#2/r2","WAHIDI-BASIT:v24p109#2/r3","WAHIDI-BASIT:v24p109/r1","WAHIDI-QT:93:7/r1","WAHIDI-WAJIZ:93:6-11/r2","WAHIDI-WAJIZ:93:6-11/r3","BAGHAWI:93:7/r2","BAGHAWI-FULL:v8p456/r2","KASHSHAF:93:7/r1","KASHSHAF:93:7/r5","KASHSHAF-FULL:v4p772#2/r5","KASHSHAF-FULL:v4p772#2/r7","IBNATIYYA:93:7/r1","IBNATIYYA:93:7/r11","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v5p494/r8","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v5p494/r15","TABRISI:93:5-7/r8","TABRISI:93:10-11/r6","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v9p158#2/r1","RAZI:93:7/r10","RAZI-FULL:v31p195#2/r6","QURTUBI:93:7/r2","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7/r2","BAYDAWI:93:7/r1","BAYDAWI-FULL:93:6-11/r2","NASAFI:93:7/r1","NASAFI-FULL:v4p345#4/r1","IBNKATHIR:93:7/r1","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v8p426/r1","BIQAI:93:7/r1","BIQAI:93:7/r2","BIQAI:93:7/r4","BIQAI-FULL:v8p457/r1","BIQAI-FULL:v8p457/r2","BIQAI-FULL:v8p457/r4","BURSEVI:v10p352#3/r3","BURSEVI:v10p352#3/r5","ALUSI:93:7/r2","ALUSI:93:7/r3","ALUSI-FULL:v30p162/r1","TABATABAI:93:8-11/r1","TABATABAI:93:8-11/r3","ELMALILI:93:6-8#2/r3","ELMALILI:93:6-8#2/r5","ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-9-english:p360/r3","STUDYQURAN:93:5/r5","STUDYQURAN:93:5/r6"],"prefer":["WAHIDI-BASIT:v24p109#2/r3","IBNATIYYA:93:7/r11","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v5p494/r15","ALUSI-FULL:v30p162/r1"],"against":["JISHUMI:v10p7457/r7"]},{"position":"He lost the road on a Syrian caravan journey with Abū Ṭālib and was returned.","reasons":"Some reports say Satan diverted the camel and Gabriel blew him to Abyssinia or India; others say a disbeliever held the reins or simply that the road was lost. Maysara is mentioned by some as Khadīja's servant. Jishumī conditions his explanation on the report's soundness.","rows":["THALABI:v10p227/r2","JISHUMI:v10p7457/r3","BAGHAWI:93:7/r5","BAGHAWI-FULL:v8p456/r5","KASHSHAF:93:7/r4","KASHSHAF-FULL:v4p772#2/r6","TABRISI:93:8/r5","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v9p158#2/r3","RAZI:93:7/r14","RAZI:93:7/r15","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r2","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r3","QURTUBI:93:7/r24","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7/r14","BAYDAWI:93:7/r2","BAYDAWI-FULL:93:6-11/r3","NASAFI:93:7/r2","NASAFI-FULL:v4p345#4/r2","ABUHAYYAN:93:7/r4","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v10p497/r3","IBNKATHIR:93:7/r3","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v8p426/r3","ALUSI:93:7/r4","ALUSI:93:7/r5","ALUSI-FULL:v30p162/r2","TABATABAI:93:8-11/r7","BINTSHATI:v1p45/r2","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:93:6/r4","STUDYQURAN:93:5/r8"],"prefer":[],"against":["ALUSI:93:7/r10","TABATABAI:93:8-11/r8"]},{"position":"He was literally lost as a child in Mecca's ravines and returned to his grandfather.","reasons":"Accounts variously name Abū Jahl, an enemy, as the rescuer; some mention sheep, a grandfather praying, hunger, a camel that would only move with Muhammad in front, and comparison with Moses preserved through an enemy. Ibn ʿAbbās is the common attribution.","rows":["THALABI:v10p225/r8","THALABI:v10p225/r9","THALABI:v10p225/r10","THALABI:v10p226/r1","JISHUMI:v10p7457/r1","BAGHAWI:93:7/r3","BAGHAWI:93:7/r4","BAGHAWI-FULL:v8p456/r3","BAGHAWI-FULL:v8p456/r4","KASHSHAF:93:7/r2","KASHSHAF-FULL:v4p772#2/r6","IBNATIYYA:93:7/r8","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v5p494/r13","TABRISI:93:8/r3","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v9p158#2/r2","RAZI:93:7/r12","RAZI:93:7/r13","RAZI-FULL:v31p195#2/r8","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r1","QURTUBI:93:7/r23","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7/r13","ABUHAYYAN:93:7/r2","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v10p497/r2","IBNKATHIR:93:7/r2","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v8p426/r2","BURSEVI:v10p353/r2","ALUSI:93:7/r6","ALUSI:93:7/r7","ALUSI-FULL:v30p162/r3","TABATABAI:93:8-11/r6","BINTSHATI:v1p45/r2","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:93:6/r3","STUDYQURAN:93:5/r7"],"prefer":[],"against":["ALUSI:93:7/r10","ALUSI-FULL:v30p162/r5","TABATABAI:93:8-11/r8","BINTSHATI:v1p46/r3"]},{"position":"Ḥalīma lost the child near Mecca after weaning, and he was recovered under a tree.","reasons":"Kaʿb's long version has an old man consult Hubal; idols fall and foretell the child's overthrow of them; ʿAbd al-Muṭṭalib prays and a heavenly voice points to a tree in Tihāma. Later summaries vary the location between gate, road, and valley.","rows":["THALABI:v10p226/r2","THALABI:v10p227/r1","JISHUMI:v10p7457/r2","KASHSHAF:93:7/r3","KASHSHAF-FULL:v4p772#2/r6","IBNATIYYA:93:7/r9","TABRISI:93:8/r4","RAZI:93:7/r11","RAZI-FULL:v31p195#2/r7","QURTUBI:93:7/r25","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7/r15","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7#2/r1","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7#2/r2","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7#2/r3","BAYDAWI:93:7/r2","BAYDAWI-FULL:93:6-11/r3","ABUHAYYAN:93:7/r3","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v10p497/r3","ALUSI:93:7/r8","ALUSI:93:7/r9","ALUSI-FULL:v30p162/r4","ALUSI-FULL:v30p162/r5","TABATABAI:93:8-11/r5","BINTSHATI:v1p45/r2"],"prefer":[],"against":["ALUSI:93:7/r10","TABATABAI:93:8-11/r8"]},{"position":"The astray ones were his people; God guided them through him.","reasons":"The addressee may stand for his people. Abū Ḥayyān parses an omitted possessive as in 'ask the town' (12:82); al-Ālūsī dismisses his dream presentation, and Bint al-Shāṭiʾ says the ellipsis is unnecessary. Al-Ḥasan's explanatory readings express guidance through the Prophet, though the transmitted verbal form varies.","rows":["SULAMI:v2p400#5/r2","THALABI:v10p225/r6","MAWARDI:93:7/r3","MAWARDI:93:7/r10","MAWARDI-FULL:v6p294/r1","QUSHAYRI:v3p429/r13","RAZI:93:7/r21","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r8","QURTUBI:93:7/r3","QURTUBI:93:7/r4","QURTUBI:93:7/r21","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7/r3","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7/r4","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7#3/r2","ABUHAYYAN:93:7/r6","ABUHAYYAN:93:7/r7","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v10p497/r4","BURSEVI:v10p352#3/r6","ALUSI:93:7/r17","ALUSI-FULL:v30p162/r9","BINTSHATI:v1p45/r3","SAMARRAI-LAMASAT-HALAQAT:p416/r5"],"prefer":["ABUHAYYAN:93:7/r6","ABUHAYYAN:93:7/r7","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v10p497/r4"],"against":["ALUSI:93:7/r18","ALUSI-FULL:v30p162/r9","BINTSHATI:v1p47/r2"]},{"position":"He was hidden, unknown, or unrecognized among his people until God made him and his worth known.","reasons":"Al-Tirmidhī and ʿAbd al-ʿAzīz ibn Yaḥyā emphasize public recognition. Al-Rāzī likens concealment to water submerged in milk; al-Sulamī describes hidden prophethood and divine favors becoming manifest. Some say Muslims were guided to recognize him.","rows":["SULAMI:v2p402/r1","SULAMI:v2p402/r8","SULAMI:v2p402/r11","THALABI:v10p228/r3","MAWARDI:93:7/r8","QUSHAYRI:v3p429/r14","JISHUMI:v10p7457/r6","IBNATIYYA:93:7/r10","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v5p494/r14","TABRISI:93:8/r6","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v9p158#2/r6","RAZI:93:7/r16","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r4","QURTUBI:93:7/r9","QURTUBI:93:7/r22","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7/r10","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7#3/r3","TABATABAI:93:8-11/r4"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"He was amid misguided people, and God guided him away from their error to monotheism and prophethood.","reasons":"Al-Farrāʾ, al-Kalbī, Ibn ʿAbbās, and others take the preposition 'among' as implied. Some reports say he seemed one of them, or that God rescued him from their misguidance, while declining to say he held it.","rows":["FARRA:v3p273#2/r7","TAB:93:6/r4","TAB:93:7/r4","TAB-FULL:v24p489/r3","THALABI:v10p225/r6","IBNATIYYA:93:7/r5","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v5p494/r11","IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p408/r7","IBNJAWZI-ZAD:v9p158#2/r4","DURR:93:7/r1","DURR-FULL:v8p544/r3","QURTUBI:93:7/r18","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7#2/r12","KASHSHAF:93:7/r5"],"prefer":[],"against":["IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p408/r8"]},{"position":"He had followed his people's way or religion for forty years before guidance.","reasons":"Al-Suddī's forty years recur in al-Ṭabarī and later sources; al-Zajjāj reports a group saying so. Sources differ whether 'way' means customary life or unbelief. Al-Qurṭubī limits the claim to outward customs without idolatry; Ibn ʿAṭiyya names customs he shared and others he opposed, including the report from Zayd ibn ʿAmr.","rows":["TAB:93:6/r3","TAB:93:7/r3","TAB-FULL:v24p489/r2","ZAJJAJ:v5p339#2/r2","THALABI:v10p225/r5","WAHIDI-BASIT:v24p109#2/r5","IBNATIYYA:93:7/r3","IBNATIYYA:93:7/r6","IBNATIYYA:93:7/r7","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v5p494/r10","RAZI:93:7/r4","RAZI-FULL:v31p195#2/r1","QURTUBI:93:7/r15","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7#2/r10","BINTSHATI:v1p44/r3"],"prefer":[],"against":["KASHSHAF:93:7/r6","KASHSHAF-FULL:v4p772#2/r8","QURTUBI:93:7/r16","QURTUBI:93:7/r17","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7#2/r11"]},{"position":"He was in love or desired guidance, and God led him to the beloved or to service of God.","reasons":"Versions differ: love for Abū Ṭālib redirected to God, already loving God, loving guidance, or longing for gnosis. Al-Rāzī cites Jacob's love (12:95) and says law enabled service of the Beloved.","rows":["THALABI:v10p228/r5","MAWARDI:93:7/r9","RAZI:93:7/r26","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r13","QURTUBI:93:7/r10","QURTUBI:93:7/r19","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7/r11","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7#2/r5","SULAMI:v2p400#5/r8","SULAMI:v2p400#5/r11","QUSHAYRI:v3p429/r13"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"He had been an unbeliever or astray from religious truth before God guided him.","reasons":"The reported literal reading invokes 42:52, 12:3, 39:65 and 28:86; Mujāhid says he lacked guidance to his religion. Al-Kalbī is reported variously as finding him an unbeliever among an unbelieving people or guiding him from their error to monotheism. The Study Quran reports the literal reading.","rows":["WAHIDI-BASIT:v24p109#2/r4","WAHIDI-BASIT:v24p109#2/r6","RAZI:93:7/r1","RAZI:93:7/r2","RAZI:93:7/r3","RAZI:93:7/r5","RAZI-FULL:v31p195#2/r1","RAZI-FULL:v31p195#2/r2","STUDYQURAN:93:5/r4","BAGHAWI:93:7/r1","BAGHAWI-FULL:v8p456/r1","MUQATIL:93:7-11/r1","IBNJAWZI-NUZHA:v1p408/r6"],"prefer":[],"against":["KASHSHAF:93:7/r6","KASHSHAF-FULL:v4p772#2/r8","NASAFI:93:7/r3","NASAFI-FULL:v4p345#4/r2","ABUHAYYAN:93:7/r1","ABUHAYYAN:93:7/r5","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v10p497/r1","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v10p497/r3","QURTUBI:93:7/r16","QURTUBI:93:7/r17","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7#2/r11","BINTSHATI:v1p44/r4","BINTSHATI:v1p46/r2","IBNASHUR:93:7/r3"]},{"position":"He lacked knowledge of the particular prophethood and future rank appointed for him.","reasons":"Al-Ṭabarī and al-Thaʿlabī describe a state unlike his later one; al-Māwardī specifies absence of prophethood. Al-Rāzī says neither he nor Jews and Christians expected a prophet outside Israel. Al-Qurṭubī calls the state unawareness of what prophethood would mean for him.","rows":["TAB:93:6/r2","TAB:93:7/r2","THALABI:v10p225/r4","MAWARDI:93:7/r2","RAZI:93:7/r20","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r7","QURTUBI:93:7/r1","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7/r1","STUDYQURAN:93:5/r3"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"He was perplexed and seeking the right way before revelation, while rejecting the surrounding religions.","reasons":"Ibn ʿAṭiyya says he followed no one's creed but searched and reflected; Ibn ʿĀshūr says God inspired rejection of polytheism to prepare him. Elmalılı says pagan, Jewish, and Christian practices did not disclose the true law. Bint al-Shāṭiʾ and Islahi cite 42:52 and 12:3; Islahi places the search at Ḥirāʾ and compares Ḥanīf seekers. Qutb says revelation resolved confusion.","rows":["IBNATIYYA:93:7/r2","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v5p494/r9","IBNASHUR:93:7/r2","BINTSHATI:v1p46/r5","BINTSHATI:v1p47/r1","ELMALILI:93:6-8#2/r4","QUTB-ZILAL:93:7/r2","QUTB-ZILAL:93:8/r2","ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-9-english:p360/r1","ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-9-english:p360/r2","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:93:6/r5"],"prefer":["IBNATIYYA:93:7/r2","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v5p494/r9","BINTSHATI:v1p46/r5","KURANYOLU-TEFSIR:93:6/r5"],"against":[]},{"position":"He was confused about explaining the revealed Book, and God taught him its explanation.","reasons":"Al-Junayd and others cite 16:44 and 16:64; al-Ālūsī calls this close to the main explanation.","rows":["SULAMI:v2p400#5/r14","THALABI:v10p228/r9","MAWARDI:93:7/r7","QURTUBI:93:7/r8","QURTUBI:93:7/r12","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7/r9","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7#2/r7","ALUSI:93:7/r12","ALUSI-FULL:v30p162/r6"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"He sought the qibla, uncertain whether the Kaʿba would be granted, and God directed him there.","reasons":"Al-Māwardī derives 'seeking' from a lost person's search; al-Rāzī and al-Qurṭubī cite 2:144 and the Prophet turning his face heavenward.","rows":["THALABI:v10p228/r11","MAWARDI:93:7/r6","RAZI:93:7/r24","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r11","QURTUBI:93:7/r7","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7/r7","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7/r8","BINTSHATI:v1p44/r5"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"He stood uniquely amid a barren religious setting, like a lone tree by which others find their way.","reasons":"Al-Thaʿlabī and al-Jishumī cite desert-tree usage; al-Rāzī calls him the tree bearing faith's fruit; al-Qurṭubī prefers this as gathering the figurative readings. Al-Ālūsī says people were guided to him.","rows":["THALABI:v10p228/r2","JISHUMI:v10p7457/r5","RAZI:93:7/r17","RAZI:93:7/r18","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r5","QURTUBI:93:7/r13","QURTUBI:93:7/r14","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7#2/r8","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7#2/r9","ALUSI:93:7/r11"],"prefer":["QURTUBI:93:7/r13","QURTUBI:93:7/r14","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7#2/r9"],"against":[]},{"position":"He sought or was uncertain about emigration until God guided his departure.","reasons":"Al-Rāzī adds Umm Maʿbad and Surāqa; Bint al-Shāṭiʾ rejects the chronology because emigration followed this early sūra by years.","rows":["MAWARDI:93:7/r4","RAZI:93:7/r23","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r10","QURTUBI:93:7/r5","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7/r5","BINTSHATI:v1p44/r5"],"prefer":[],"against":["BINTSHATI:v1p45/r1"]},{"position":"He did not know the heavenly route during the Night Journey and God guided him onward.","reasons":"Versions say Gabriel left him before the Lote Tree or Throne; al-Rāzī also describes guidance through the heavenly paths.","rows":["THALABI:v10p228/r1","JISHUMI:v10p7457/r4","RAZI:93:7/r29","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r16","QURTUBI:93:7/r26","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7#2/r4"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"He did not yet know himself or his true identity, and God disclosed it.","reasons":"Bassām ibn ʿAbd Allāh and parallel reports describe self-recognition, not a change of religion.","rows":["THALABI:v10p228/r4","BAGHAWI:93:7/r6","BAGHAWI-FULL:v8p456/r6","QURTUBI:93:7/r11","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7#2/r6"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"He lacked worldly livelihood or trade knowledge and God showed him its means.","reasons":"Abū Muslim connects livelihood to a Prophetic saying on provision through jihad; al-Rāzī says profitable trade attracted Khadīja. Bint al-Shāṭiʾ objects both on chronology and on its inadequacy as the favor intended.","rows":["TABRISI:93:8/r1","RAZI:93:7/r27","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r14","BINTSHATI:v1p44/r5"],"prefer":[],"against":["BINTSHATI:v1p45/r1","BINTSHATI:v1p46/r3"]},{"position":"He forgot a promised qualification, and God reminded him.","reasons":"The reports concern the Companions of the Cave, Dhū al-Qarnayn, and the Spirit; 2:282 supplies the forgetting sense of ḍalla.","rows":["THALABI:v10p228/r7","QURTUBI:93:7/r6","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7/r6"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"He was powerless and mistreated, until God made him a leader over his people.","reasons":"Al-Rāzī says they would not even accept him as a subordinate before divine strengthening.","rows":["RAZI:93:7/r28","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r15"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"He did not initially recognize Gabriel at his first appearance, then God enabled recognition.","reasons":"Al-Rāzī adds that the first appearance frightened him.","rows":["RAZI:93:7/r25","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r12"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"He was separated from the misguided and later guided to mix with and summon them.","reasons":"Al-Rāzī says being 'lost' can mean apart from their religion. Another proposal says he already knew God inwardly but did not publicly oppose his people.","rows":["RAZI:93:7/r22","RAZI:93:7/r31","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r9"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"He was away from the laws but never spiritually without God's guidance.","reasons":"Al-Ṭabāṭabāʾī says every creature has no intrinsic guidance apart from God's gift, which accompanied the Prophet from the beginning.","rows":["TABATABAI:93:3-7/r9"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Whether it describes missing revealed knowledge, searching, prior religious error, his people, obscurity, literal loss, or a specific unmet need."}
{"question":"What is affirmed or denied about the Prophet's pre-prophetic belief and protection?","words":["ضَآلًّۭا"],"type":"theology","positions":[{"position":"The Prophet never practiced shirk or unbelief; ḍāllan cannot impute it to him.","reasons":"Al-Kashshāf invokes prophetic protection and 12:38; al-Rāzī cites 53:2. Ibn ʿAṭiyya says he never worshipped an idol despite some shared customs. Al-Biqāʿī says sound reason and primordial nature led him to monotheism; al-ʿĀshūr cites his public life in 10:16 and 23:69. Bint al-Shāṭiʾ reports the majority rejection; Elmalılı stresses his reputation for trustworthiness.","rows":["WAHIDI-BASIT:v24p109#2/r2","WAHIDI-BASIT:v24p109#2/r7","JISHUMI:v10p7459/r16","KASHSHAF:93:7/r6","KASHSHAF-FULL:v4p772#2/r8","IBNATIYYA:93:7/r6","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v5p494/r12","RAZI:93:7/r6","RAZI:93:7/r9","RAZI-FULL:v31p195#2/r3","RAZI-FULL:v31p195#2/r5","QURTUBI:93:7/r16","QURTUBI:93:7/r17","QURTUBI-FULL:93:7#2/r11","NASAFI:93:7/r3","NASAFI-FULL:v4p345#4/r2","ABUHAYYAN:93:7/r1","ABUHAYYAN:93:7/r5","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v10p497/r1","ABUHAYYAN-FULL:v10p497/r3","BIQAI:93:7/r1","BIQAI:93:7/r3","BIQAI-FULL:v8p457/r1","BIQAI-FULL:v8p457/r3","BURSEVI:v10p352#3/r5","ELMALILI:93:6-8#2/r1","ELMALILI:93:6-8#2/r2","IBNASHUR:93:7/r3","IBNASHUR:93:7/r5","IBNASHUR:93:7/r6","BINTSHATI:v1p44/r4","BINTSHATI:v1p46/r2","STUDYQURAN:93:5/r5","STUDYQURAN:93:5/r6"],"prefer":["RAZI:93:7/r6"],"against":["RAZI:93:7/r1","RAZI:93:7/r3"]},{"position":"Prior unbelief is rationally possible in general, but transmitted evidence excludes it here.","reasons":"Al-Wāḥidī's school requires transmission to decide and even allows rational possibility of later faith and prophethood or removal of prophethood; al-Rāzī's school cites 53:2 to settle this case.","rows":["WAHIDI-BASIT:v24p109#2/r7","RAZI:93:7/r8","RAZI:93:7/r9","RAZI-FULL:v31p195#2/r5"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"A reported Sunni interpretation links the verse to the Prophet's pre-Islamic arrangement of his daughter's marriage.","reasons":"Ibn ʿAṭiyya transmits Thaʿlab's attribution without developing its argument; the full version repeats it.","rows":["IBNATIYYA:93:7/r12","IBNATIYYA-FULL:v5p494/r16"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"God protected the young Prophet from intentions to join Meccan entertainments.","reasons":"ʿAlī's report says he twice intended to join nighttime wedding festivities, slept until sunrise each time, and never again intended evil before revelation.","rows":["RAZI:93:7/r32","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r18","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r19"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Prior unbelief is rationally impossible because it would repel people from the Prophet.","reasons":"Al-Rāzī attributes this rationale to the Muʿtazila.","rows":["RAZI:93:7/r7","RAZI-FULL:v31p195#2/r4"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The extent of pre-prophetic protection from grave indecent sins is disputed.","reasons":"Ibn ʿĀshūr says strict scholars of his school exempt prophets from them, while the Muʿtazila deny such immunity because reason itself identifies grave wrongs.","rows":["IBNASHUR:93:7/r4"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Whether unbelief was possible in reason, and what scripture and prophetic protection establish about this case."}
{"question":"How does 'fa-hadā' describe God's guidance?","words":["فَهَدَىٰ"],"type":"interpretation","positions":[{"position":"God guided the Prophet by revelation to scripture, law, monotheism, and the straight way.","reasons":"Gabriel's gloss is guidance to God's religion; al-Bayḍāwī, al-Biqāʿī, and others add inspiration and sound reflection. Ibn Kathīr quotes 42:52. Al-Ṭabrisī speaks of reason, proofs, and divine favor amid idolatry; Elmalılı emphasizes divine selection and teaching.","rows":["MUQATIL:93:7-11/r1","TABRISI:93:8/r2","KASHSHAF:93:7/r5","KASHSHAF-FULL:v4p772#2/r7","BAYDAWI:93:7/r1","BAYDAWI-FULL:93:6-11/r2","BIQAI:93:7/r4","BIQAI-FULL:v8p457/r4","IBNKATHIR:93:7/r1","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v8p426/r1","ELMALILI:93:6-8#2/r5","ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-9-english:p360/r3","QUTB-ZILAL:93:7/r2","QUTB-ZILAL:93:8/r2"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The verb 'guided' omits its understood object for concision and verse ending rhyme.","reasons":"Al-Thaʿlabī, al-Ālūsī, and Ibn ʿĀshūr derive the object from the second-person pronoun; Bint al-Shāṭiʾ argues the open wording extends to those guided through his mission. Al-Ālūsī objects that the following fa-clauses resist a proposed expansive-generosity account.","rows":["THALABI:v10p229/r5","ALUSI:93:8/r18","ALUSI:93:8/r19","IBNASHUR:93:8/r3","IBNASHUR-FULL:v30p396/r3","IBNASHUR-FULL:v30p400/r2","BINTSHATI:v1p51/r4"],"prefer":[],"against":["ALUSI:93:8/r19"]},{"position":"God guided others through the Prophet and his revealed law.","reasons":"Ibn ʿAṭāʾ makes him a means of guidance to the lost. Al-Sāmarrāʾī cites 42:52, saying the Prophet too guides to a straight path.","rows":["SULAMI:v2p400#5/r1","SULAMI:v2p400#5/r2","RAZI:93:7/r21","RAZI-FULL:v31p196/r8","SAMARRAI-LAMASAT-HALAQAT:p416/r5"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"This is likely the earliest Qurʾānic use of hadā and resonates with scriptural guidance motifs.","reasons":"Corpus Coranicum notes biblical and psalmic parallels for personal divine guidance and Syriac dbar as a technical parallel, while listing later Qurʾānic occurrences.","rows":["CORPUSCORANICUM:93:7:anmerkung/r1"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"The object and mode of guidance vary across the preceding readings."}
{"question":"How is this clause joined grammatically to the preceding question?","words":["وَوَجَدَكَ","فَهَدَىٰ"],"type":"grammar","positions":[{"position":"It coordinates with the affirmative sense implied by the rhetorical question.","reasons":"Al-Burhān says a rhetorical question of report is affirmative, allowing an affirmative clause to join it, with parallels 94:1 and 105:2. Al-Ālūsī and Elmalılı allow this reading.","rows":["BURHAN:v2p331/r7","ALUSI:93:7/r1","ALUSI-FULL:v30p162/r1","ELMALILI:93:6-8/r14"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"It coordinates directly with the prior verb under lam's negation.","reasons":"Al-Ālūsī and Elmalılı give this as the other syntactic possibility; Ibn ʿĀshūr describes the rhetorical question as expecting assent.","rows":["ALUSI:93:7/r1","ELMALILI:93:6-8/r14","IBNASHUR-FULL:v30p398/r5"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Whether coordination follows the affirmative implication or the earlier negated verb."}
{"question":"What inward meaning is drawn from the Prophet's being lost and guided?","words":["ضَآلًّۭا","فَهَدَىٰ"],"type":"inward","positions":[{"position":"He sought pure divine love and was guided to gnosis through nearness and affection.","reasons":"Tustarī and al-Sulamī describe a cup of nearness, knowledge of God's love, and pre-eternal love; Jaʿfar says God made Himself known. Other voices describe longing and ecstatic bewilderment. Al-Najmiyya describes passage from annihilation to sobriety and complete knowledge.","rows":["TUSTARI:93:7/r1","SULAMI:v2p400#5/r4","SULAMI:v2p400#5/r7","SULAMI:v2p400#5/r8","SULAMI:v2p400#5/r9","SULAMI:v2p400#5/r11","SULAMI:v2p400#5/r13","SULAMI:v2p403/r1","QUSHAYRI:v3p429/r12","QUSHAYRI:v3p429/r13","QUSHAYRI:v3p429/r14","THALABI:v10p228/r5","BURSEVI:v10p353/r1","ALUSI:93:7/r14","ALUSI:93:7/r15","ALUSI:93:7/r16","ALUSI-FULL:v30p162/r7","ALUSI-FULL:v30p162/r8"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"He was unaware of his own worth, and God disclosed his noble rank.","reasons":"Sahl and other voices in al-Sulamī, al-Thaʿlabī, and al-Ālūsī describe unveiling of status and the signs of prophethood.","rows":["TUSTARI:93:7/r1","SULAMI:v2p400#5/r12","SULAMI:v2p403/r1","THALABI:v10p228/r4","QUSHAYRI:v3p429/r14","ALUSI:93:7/r13","ALUSI-FULL:v30p162/r7"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"He was bewildered in witnessing divine transcendence, then guided by God to God.","reasons":"Al-Qushayrī and al-Najmiyya describe divine bewilderment followed by knowledge or sobriety; al-Wāsiṭī warns ordinary categories of misguidance and guidance do not apply to his supreme rank.","rows":["SULAMI:v2p402/r3","SULAMI:v2p402/r4","QUSHAYRI:v3p429/r12","BURSEVI:v10p353/r1","QASHANI:93:4-8/r4"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The natural self lacked the path to gnosis and was redirected toward knowledge and law.","reasons":"Tustarī and Sahl describe the soul's poverty; al-Thaʿlabī adds desire as its motive.","rows":["TUSTARI:93:7/r2","SULAMI:v2p400#5/r5","THALABI:v10p228/r8"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"He moved from inference and conventional forms to direct recognition.","reasons":"Bandār says divine recognition made proofs and witnesses unnecessary; Ibn ʿAṭāʾ says he was lost from convention, not gnosis.","rows":["SULAMI:v2p402/r5","SULAMI:v2p402/r6","THALABI:v10p228/r10"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"A person's search for their virtue ends when God reveals it.","reasons":"Al-Sulamī transmits this more general inward application.","rows":["SULAMI:v2p402/r10"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Which spiritual lack, bewilderment, or transition the inward reading describes."}
{"question":"How do these remembered favors connect to the sūra's reassurance and duties?","words":["*"],"type":"links","positions":[{"position":"Past shelter, guidance, and enrichment reassure the Prophet that God has not abandoned him and will continue His care.","reasons":"Al-Ṭabarī frames the passage as blessings; Qatāda calls these the Prophet's prior circumstances. Ibn ʿĀshūr says steady care from childhood proves the promise and warns opponents. Elmalılı says each later state surpasses the former; Qutb stresses guidance's comfort during the pause; Bint al-Shāṭiʾ, Islahi, and Study Quran describe the same movement through the sūra.","rows":["TAB:93:7/r2","TAB:93:8/r4","IBNKATHIR:93:8/r2","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v8p426/r5","IBNASHUR-FULL:v30p398/r4","ELMALILI:93:6-8#2/r11","QUTB-ZILAL:93:7/r3","QUTB-ZILAL:93:8/r3","BINTSHATI:v1p42/r1","ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-9-english:p353/r2","ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-9-english:p354/r1","STUDYQURAN:93:intro/r6","BINTSHATI:v1p51/r2"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The matching response is to shelter orphans, guide seekers, provide help, and remember God's enabling favor.","reasons":"Al-Rāzī calls benefaction an unrepayable lifelong debt; service of orphans and petitioners depends on God's help. Al-Ṭabrisī and al-Itqān connect teaching a seeker with the Prophet's being taught; the sūra closes with duties corresponding to favors.","rows":["RAZI:93:6/r11","RAZI:93:6/r12","RAZI-FULL:v31p194#3/r6","RAZI-FULL:v31p194#3/r7","RAZI-FULL:v31p198/r8","TABRISI:93:10-11/r6","ITQAN:v3p251#2/r7","STUDYQURAN:93:intro/r6","ISLAHI-TADABBUR:vol-9-english:p354/r1"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The sequence of conditions and responses is a matched listing of divine favors.","reasons":"Al-Itqān defines laf wa-nashr as listing items and matching details; al-Itqān also notes the metaphor of life as guidance. Ibn ʿĀshūr reads the three states as aid to someone in distress.","rows":["ITQAN:v3p251#2/r1","ITQAN:v3p124/r3","IBNASHUR-FULL:v30p398/r5"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Sūrah 94 echoes the grammatical form and extends the blessings of Sūrah 93.","reasons":"Study Quran says 94:1 follows 93:6's construction, implied in 93:7–8; Sūrah 93 addresses external needs and Sūrah 94 spiritual relief.","rows":["STUDYQURAN:94:intro/r4","STUDYQURAN:94:intro/r5"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The sūra and Psalm 13 share lament and reassurance motifs.","reasons":"Corpus Coranicum identifies structural and thematic links with psalmic lament prayers.","rows":["CORPUSCORANICUM:93:7:anmerkung/r2"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"How past care supports promised future care and the later commands."}
{"question":"What report has God remind the Prophet of these favors?","words":["*"],"type":"reports","positions":[{"position":"After the Prophet asked about favors given to earlier prophets, God recalled shelter, guidance, enrichment, and other gifts.","reasons":"The reports vary: comparison with Abraham, Moses, David, or Solomon; opened breast, removed burden, raised renown, al-Baqara's closing verses, and divine friendship. He answers each reminder with praise or affirmation. Ibn Abī Ḥātim, al-Baghawī, al-Rāzī, al-Durr, Ibn Kathīr, al-Nashr, and Jishumī preserve variants.","rows":["MUQATIL:93:7-11/r1","IBNABIHATIM:v10p3443#2/r2","IBNABIHATIM:v10p3445#3/r4","IBNABIHATIM:v10p3445/r1","JISHUMI:v10p7452/r2","JISHUMI:v10p7452/r3","BAGHAWI:93:6/r2","BAGHAWI-FULL:v8p455/r5","RAZI:93:8/r22","IBNKATHIR-FULL:v8p430/r6","NASHR:v2p409/r1","DURR:93:6/r5","DURR-FULL:v8p543/r7"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The request-for-gifts version is implausible because prophets ask only by permission.","reasons":"Al-Qāḍī objects that a permitted request must be answered and a reproachful reply would be inappropriate; Jishumī calls Night Journey gift stories unsound.","rows":["RAZI:93:8/r23","RAZI-FULL:v31p198/r9","JISHUMI:v10p7452/r11"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The pause served a benefit or a period of preparation.","reasons":"Jishumī argues a proven pause must benefit believers; Ibn ʿĀshūr proposes respite and capacity for arduous revelation. A report has Gabriel say he descends only by divine command.","rows":["JISHUMI:v10p7452/r9","JISHUMI:v10p7452/r10","IBNASHUR-FULL:v30p396/r1","IBNASHUR-FULL:v30p396/r2"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The revelation pause lasted twelve, fifteen, twenty-five, or forty days in differing reports.","reasons":"Jishumī names Ibn Jurayj for twelve, Ibn ʿAbbās for fifteen, an unnamed report for twenty-five, and Muqātil for forty. Ibn ʿĀshūr suggests two pauses, forty then about twelve days, with this sūra the third revelation, to reconcile reports.","rows":["JISHUMI:v10p7452/r5","JISHUMI:v10p7452/r6","JISHUMI:v10p7452/r7","JISHUMI:v10p7452/r8","IBNASHUR-FULL:v30p396/r1","IBNASHUR-FULL:v30p396/r2"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"The Prophet was already noble despite orphanhood and modest Arab wealth.","reasons":"Ibn ʿAmr explains the parents' loss and limited wealth while discussing Abū Ṭālib's poetry.","rows":["IBNABIHATIM:v10p3443#2/r1"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Takbīr at the end suits the sūra's account of divine favors.","reasons":"Al-Nashr gives this rationale.","rows":["NASHR:v2p409/r2"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"Reciting al-Ḍuḥā brings intercession and rewards measured by orphans and petitioners.","reasons":"Al-Bayḍāwī transmits the reported virtue.","rows":["BAYDAWI-FULL:93:6-11/r8"],"prefer":[],"against":[]},{"position":"A pause of revelation was attributed to a puppy in the Prophet's home.","reasons":"Jishumī reports Gabriel saying angels do not enter a house with a dog or image.","rows":["JISHUMI:v10p7452/r1"],"prefer":[],"against":[]}],"turns_on":"Whether a report recounts comparison with earlier prophets, the request's propriety, or another setting."}
